<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castro Urdiales</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castro Urdiales is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It is identified with the Roman colony of Flaviobriga, founded under Vespasian in the territory of the Autrigones. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was an important fishing and trading port of the Hermandad de las Marismas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castro-urdiales.jpg" alt="Castro Urdiales" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asqueladd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castro_Urdiales._Cantabria.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Castro Urdiales was originally called Portus Amanum and was the chief city of the Autrigones. In 74 a Roman colony was established there under the name Flaviobriga, in the reign of Vespasian, most likely to mine the abundant iron of the area. A Roman milestone opposite the Church of Santa María dates to 61, and the water channel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-chorrillo">El Chorrillo</a> is a Roman work of hydraulic engineering.</p><p>The town received its municipal charter in 1163. It lay at the western tip of the coastline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> until 1200, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> invaded the kingdom. It profited from its place at a crossroads of trade between northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Castile. It remained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a> until at least 1476, and until the 18th century it tried to rejoin Biscay, attempts that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> refused because they could tip the balance against its own trade. The French destroyed the town in 1813, but it was rebuilt and fortified. Its growth dates from iron mining and railways after 1879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Castro Urdiales is a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a>. Its chief industries are tourism, fishing and the oil-packing of fish, especially sardines and anchovies. The old quarter, the Puebla Vieja, was declared a Conjunto histórico in 1978. The Gothic church of Santa María de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> was built in the 13th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-viii">Alfonso VIII</a>, finished in the 15th and declared a National Monument in 1931. The Castle of Santa Ana stands near the port. The Cave of El Cuco holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paleolithic">Paleolithic</a> rock carvings and paintings of deer, goats and horses.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castro Urdiales” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Autrigones occupied the land from the upper Ebro to the coast around <b>Castro Urdiales</b>, in modern Burgos, Álava and Bizkaia, from the later Iron Age.</blockquote><a href="/state/autrigones">Autrigones</a></li></ul></section>
